A||N||7||odis||buying a fiero, need information||||1||
Z||000000||odis||04-09-2001||12:32 AM||||i am considering purchasing an 84 pontiac fiero.  it is an automatic with 80,000 miles on it.  It will pass inspection and it runs good.   What i want to know is are these cars reliable.  Are there any known problems with these cars breaking down or anything like that?  Any head problems?  If someone would please let me know the track record of this car it would be most appreciated.  thankyou in advance.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Hank is Here||04-09-2001||12:42 AM||||odis, <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">welcome to the Forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>About your questions.  The 2.5 is not a powerhouse but a good economy engine.  In 84 there was a problem with bad connecting rods in early cars, this was resolved with recalls.  Now sure way to tell if they were done but I would look at the front on the truck lid when open if there is a rubber strip between the firewall and the lid chances are it was not done, this is only a rough was.  <BR>Reliability of the car is really that of any car that is 16 years old.  Mainly it depends on maintenance, I on my GT I have only needed to replace a headlight motor and temp sending unit in a year, bringing my bill for a year under $100.  <BR>If you don't mind me asking, where are you, what is the general condition of the car, and the asking price--all helpful in determining if it is worth it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||jch82||04-09-2001||09:53 AM||||First off, welcome! These are the funnest little cars you could ever want to drive. <P>There are many things you can modify and change around.<P>Like Hank says, it is a 16yr old car and it all depends on how well previous owners maintained it....<P>Anyways Welcome  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Yeah, my girlfriend takes collect calls from the road<BR>And it doesn't seem to matter that I'm lacking in the bulge<BR>She laughs at my dumb jokes when no one does<BR>She brings me mexican food from Sombrero's just because<BR>                 <I>-Blink 182</I>||||reg||14
Z||000003||ChadMan||04-09-2001||02:34 PM||||If your only worry is reliability, I'd go ahead and buy it, Odis.  The Iron Duke is noisy and slow, but very reliable, and the TH-125 is a good transmission.  You might have to replace some nickel-and-dime stuff like sensors, fusible links, etc, but if that Fiero has been cared for, you shouldn't have any drivetrain problems.<P>The only chronic problem I know of with the Iron Duke is that the valve guides on the earlier ones started leaking by 90K miles or so.  This is nothing serious - it just means the car will burn a little oil when you first fire it up and under deceleration.  You'll still have good compression and the car will run like that indefinitely.<P>When you go to test drive it, have a buddy gun it a time or two and watch for blue smoke.  If you see some, act scared for the engine's health and try to haggle the owner down a couple hundred bucks or so.  Worked for me...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jay||04-09-2001||04:09 PM||||Welcome,<BR>My first real car was an 84 Fiero (My first one was a Lada).  I did have a problem with the head.  There was a crack in it and it needed to be redone.  A quick way to check is to look in the oil cap or radiator cap.  If you see a white foam, stay away.  Other than that, I loved my 84.  Almost 10 years later I bought my 86 GT and did the Northstar Conversion.  It's a great car to play around with!  Go for it!!  Then again what did you expect, this is a fiero forum!!!<P>1986 Fiero GT Northstar <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||odis||04-10-2001||12:23 AM||||thank you everyone, for the advice.  the car aint the prettiest, but it seems to run good and i am buying it tomorrow.||||reg||1
